{"reason": "The initial plan's core direction is to reduce high fold variance (0.3068 ± 0.0185) and early overfitting (best epochs at 2-3) through multi-sample dropout, SWA, token cutoff, feature masking, and differential layer warmup. Historical successful experiments (e.g., planned 5-fold ensemble with reduced LR, increased warmup; gradient accumulation with layer-wise decay; feature-level mixup with back-translation) strongly support the use of multi-sample dropout and advanced regularization to stabilize predictions. Conversely, complex pseudo-labeling and certain augmentations (SSMix, DropPath) failed or hurt performance. I choose data_processing_and_feature_engineering to add token-level cutoff (informed by successful augmentation strategies that preserve stylometric patterns), model_design to implement multi-sample dropout in the classifier head (proven effective in multiple successful historical plans), and training_evaluation to add SWA with differential layer warmup (directly addresses the root cause of poor generalization and high variance, aligning with successful LR schedule adjustments from historical experiments). These modules address training robustness (multi-sample dropout, SWA) and data diversity (token cutoff) without introducing harmful augmentations.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Implement token-level cutoff augmentation in the AuthorDataset class. During training (when labels are not None), with 50% probability, randomly mask 15% of tokens (using the tokenizer's mask token) in the tokenized sequence before encoding. This is done after tokenization but before padding: if the number of tokens exceeds the max_length, truncate first, then apply token-level masking by replacing some tokens with tokenizer.mask_token_id. The augmentation is applied only during training and should be performed inside __getitem__ after tokenization. Preserve the existing word-dropout augmentation (0.1 prob) as a complementary technique. This change forces the model to rely on contextual clues from surrounding tokens, improving robustness against overfitting to exact token patterns. Historical successful experiments using random word deletion and back-translation show that moderate token-level noise improves generalization; token masking is a lighter, proven alternative that avoids the computational cost of back-translation and the pitfalls of SSMix.", "model_design": "Implement multi-sample dropout in the MultiPoolingDeBERTa's forward method. During training, when model is in train mode, compute K=5 stochastic forward passes through the classifier head (only the dropout-applying part, i.e., after the concatenated pooling), average the resulting logits, and return the averaged logits. Specifically, after computing combined_pool, repeat it K times along a new batch dimension (or loop K times), apply the classifier's dropout layers (which have stochastic behavior) independently each time, then average the logits across the K samples. During evaluation, use a single forward pass. This approximates Bayesian model averaging, reducing prediction variance and improving calibration, directly addressing the observed high fold variance (0.0185). Historical successful experiments consistently show multi-sample dropout (K=4 or 5) yields 0.01-0.02 log loss improvement, while avoiding the double-application issues that plagued EMA.", "training_evaluation": "Implement Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) over the last 3 epochs and differential layer warmup. (1) SWA: After each of the last 3 training epochs, store a copy of the model state_dict; at the end of training, compute the average of these checkpoints and load it as the final model for that fold. This flattens the loss landscape and improves generalization, similar to historical successful usage of EMA but with weight averaging instead. (2) Replace the uniform warmup with a differential schedule: keep the first 18 DeBERTa layers at a constant learning rate of 1e-6 for the first 20% of total steps (no warmup), while applying a linear warmup to the last 6 layers (19-24) and the classifier head from 0 to the base LR (2e-5) over the same 20% warmup period. After warmup, apply cosine decay to all layers. This preserves pretrained lower-layer features while allowing task-specific adaptation, reducing catastrophic forgetting and overfitting. Historical successful experiments with layer-wise decay and differential LR schedules support this approach."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan's core direction is to reduce high fold variance (0.3068 ± 0.0185) and early overfitting (best epochs at 2-3) through multi-sample dropout, SWA, token cutoff, feature masking, and differential layer warmup. Historical successful experiments (e.g., planned 5-fold ensemble with reduced LR, increased warmup; gradient accumulation with layer-wise decay; feature-level mixup with back-translation) strongly support the use of multi-sample dropout and advanced regularization to stabilize predictions. Conversely, complex pseudo-labeling and certain augmentations (SSMix, DropPath) failed or hurt performance. I choose data_processing_and_feature_engineering to add token-level cutoff (informed by successful augmentation strategies that preserve stylometric patterns), model_design to implement multi-sample dropout in the classifier head (proven effective in multiple successful historical plans), and training_evaluation to add SWA with differential layer warmup (directly addresses the root cause of poor generalization and high variance, aligning with successful LR schedule adjustments from historical experiments). These modules address training robustness (multi-sample dropout, SWA) and data diversity (token cutoff) without introducing harmful augmentations.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Implement token-level cutoff augmentation in the AuthorDataset class. During training (when labels are not None), with 50% probability, randomly mask 15% of tokens (using the tokenizer's mask token) in the tokenized sequence before encoding. This is done after tokenization but before padding: if the number of tokens exceeds the max_length, truncate first, then apply token-level masking by replacing some tokens with tokenizer.mask_token_id. The augmentation is applied only during training and should be performed inside __getitem__ after tokenization. Preserve the existing word-dropout augmentation (0.1 prob) as a complementary technique. This change forces the model to rely on contextual clues from surrounding tokens, improving robustness against overfitting to exact token patterns. Historical successful experiments using random word deletion and back-translation show that moderate token-level noise improves generalization; token masking is a lighter, proven alternative that avoids the computational cost of back-translation and the pitfalls of SSMix.",
    "model_design": "Implement multi-sample dropout in the MultiPoolingDeBERTa's forward method. During training, when model is in train mode, compute K=5 stochastic forward passes through the classifier head (only the dropout-applying part, i.e., after the concatenated pooling), average the resulting logits, and return the averaged logits. Specifically, after computing combined_pool, repeat it K times along a new batch dimension (or loop K times), apply the classifier's dropout layers (which have stochastic behavior) independently each time, then average the logits across the K samples. During evaluation, use a single forward pass. This approximates Bayesian model averaging, reducing prediction variance and improving calibration, directly addressing the observed high fold variance (0.0185). Historical successful experiments consistently show multi-sample dropout (K=4 or 5) yields 0.01-0.02 log loss improvement, while avoiding the double-application issues that plagued EMA.",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) over the last 3 epochs and differential layer warmup. (1) SWA: After each of the last 3 training epochs, store a copy of the model state_dict; at the end of training, compute the average of these checkpoints and load it as the final model for that fold. This flattens the loss landscape and improves generalization, similar to historical successful usage of EMA but with weight averaging instead. (2) Replace the uniform warmup with a differential schedule: keep the first 18 DeBERTa layers at a constant learning rate of 1e-6 for the first 20% of total steps (no warmup), while applying a linear warmup to the last 6 layers (19-24) and the classifier head from 0 to the base LR (2e-5) over the same 20% warmup period. After warmup, apply cosine decay to all layers. This preserves pretrained lower-layer features while allowing task-specific adaptation, reducing catastrophic forgetting and overfitting. Historical successful experiments with layer-wise decay and differential LR schedules support this approach."
  }
}"}
